📱 Role of Each Component:
1. LAPTOP / BROWSER (User Control Interface)
What it does:

Opens a web browser and connects to master ESP32 at http://192.168.x.x:80

Displays live video feeds from 3 cameras (stereo pair + gripper view)

Shows real-time robot status (position, heading, obstacles)

Allows user to control:

Arm joint angles via sliders

Vegetable picking commands

Robot movement

Connection:

Wireless (Wi-Fi) to master ESP32

No direct physical connection needed

2. MASTER ESP32 (Central Command & Web Server)
What it does:

Web Server: Hosts the interactive control page with HTML/CSS/JavaScript

Wi-Fi Router: Connects to your home/garden Wi-Fi network

Command Center: Receives clicks/inputs from browser

Status Dashboard: Displays real-time telemetry from robot

Bridge: Routes commands to slave ESP32 and sensor data back to browser

Key Features:

text
Master ESP32 Responsibilities:
├─ Wi-Fi connectivity (auto-reconnect)
├─ HTTP server (port 80)
├─ Web UI with live video streams
├─ Arm visualization (SVG animation)
├─ Robot pose map (canvas with heading arrow)
├─ UART2 communication with slave
│  ├─ Sends: "B90 S45 E135 W90" (joint commands)
│  └─ Sends: "PICK tomato 5" (picking commands)
└─ Receives status packets (pose, heading, obstacle distance)
Physical Connections:

Power (5V/GND)

UART2 to Slave ESP32 (GPIO16 RX, GPIO17 TX)

Wi-Fi antenna

3. WEB CAMERAS (ESP32 CAM Modules) - 3x
What they do:

Stream live video to browser

Stereo Camera #1: Left camera for depth perception

Stereo Camera #2: Right camera for depth perception

Gripper Camera: Mounted on arm for close-up vegetable inspection

Specifications:

Each camera runs its own ESP32-CAM module

Each has local HTTP stream server on port 81

Master ESP32 displays their streams via <img> tags

URLs:

http://192.168.4.2:81/stream (Stereo Cam 1)

http://192.168.4.3:81/stream (Stereo Cam 2)

http://192.168.4.4:81/stream (Gripper Cam)

Connection:

Each camera connected to its own Wi-Fi network

Streams sent directly to browser (not through master ESP32)

4. SLAVE ESP32 (Sensor Fusion & Command Router)
What it does:

Sensor Reader: Continuously reads all onboard sensors

IMU (heading/orientation)

Wheel encoder (distance traveled)

Ultrasonic sensor (obstacles)

GPS (optional global position)

Odometry Engine: Computes robot position using dead reckoning

text
Position = Previous Position + Distance * cos(heading)
Command Router: Receives text commands from master, forwards to Arduino Mega

text
Master: "B90 S45 E135 W90"
   ↓
Slave parses & forwards to Mega
   ↓
Mega moves servos
Status Reporter: Sends binary packets back to master every 200ms with:

Current X, Y position

Current heading (yaw angle)

Obstacle distance

Timing:

Sensor reads: Every 100ms

Status sends: Every 200ms

Command processing: Real-time

5. ARDUINO MEGA (Real-Time Hardware Control)
What it does:

Motor Driver Interface: Generates PWM signals to control motors

Servo Controller: Precise control of 4 arm joint servos

Drive Control: Manages wheel/track motors

Gripper Control: Opens/closes vegetable gripper

Real-Time Safety: Immediate response to commands (no network delays)

Hardware Connections:

text
Arduino Mega receives commands from Slave ESP32
  ↓
Parses: "B90 S45 E135 W90" (angle values)
  ↓
Generates PWM to servos:
  ├─ Pin 5 → Base servo
  ├─ Pin 6 → Shoulder servo
  ├─ Pin 7 → Elbow servo
  └─ Pin 8 → Wrist servo
🔄 Complete Command Flow Example:
Scenario: User picks a tomato
text
1. USER (Laptop Browser)
   └─ Enters "tomato" in text field
   └─ Clicks "Start Detect & Pick" button

2. BROWSER (JavaScript)
   └─ Sends HTTP GET: /command?cmd=PICK%20tomato%205
   └─ Travels over Wi-Fi to Master ESP32

3. MASTER ESP32
   └─ Receives HTTP request
   └─ Extracts: cmd = "PICK tomato 5"
   └─ Sends via UART2 to Slave: "PICK tomato 5\n"
   └─ Returns HTTP response: "Command sent"

4. SLAVE ESP32
   └─ Reads from UART: "PICK tomato 5"
   └─ Parses: vegetable="tomato", count=5
   └─ Sends to Arduino Mega via UART1: "PICK tomato 5\n"

5. ARDUINO MEGA
   └─ Receives: "PICK tomato 5"
   └─ Triggers picking routine:
      ├─ Move arm to scanning position
      ├─ Activate computer vision on gripper camera
      ├─ Detect tomatoes in field of view
      ├─ Move gripper to first tomato
      ├─ Close gripper
      ├─ Retract arm
      └─ Repeat 5 times

6. WHILE PICKING - Real-time feedback
   └─ Slave ESP32 continuously reads sensors
   └─ Every 200ms: sends status packet to Master
   └─ Master receives: position, heading, obstacles
   └─ Browser updates: robot position on map

7. USER (Laptop Browser)
   └─ Sees robot move in real-time on map
   └─ Sees arm kinematics update
   └─ Sees camera feeds showing picking action
📊 Data Communication Diagram
text
┌─ COMMAND FLOW (Text) ──────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                              │
│  Browser                Master ESP32         Slave ESP32    │
│  (HTTP GET)  ──────>   (HTTP Server)  ────>  (UART Text)   │
│  "B90 S45..." :        receives       :      parses         │
│                        routes to      :      forwards        │
│                        slave          :      to Mega        │
│                                                              │
├─ STATUS FLOW (Binary) ─────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                              │
│  Arduino Mega          Slave ESP32          Master ESP32    │
│  (Executing)  :<────   (Binary Packet)  <──  (HTTP JSON)   │
│               :        0xAA 18 01 ...       Browser reads    │
│               :        sends every 200ms    position, etc   │
│                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
🎯 Key Points to Remember
Component	Role	Connection	Timing
Laptop	User interface & monitoring	Wi-Fi (wireless)	Interactive
Master ESP32	Web server & command router	Wi-Fi + UART2	Always on
Cameras (3x)	Live video streaming	Wi-Fi (wireless)	Continuous 15-30fps
Slave ESP32	Sensor fusion & command router	UART2 + UART1	100-200ms updates
Arduino Mega	Hardware control	UART1	Real-time (ms)
Sensors	Data collection	I2C/GPIO/Serial	100ms read cycle
Motors/Servos	Physical actuation	PWM/Digital	Real-time response
💡 System Design Philosophy
Layered Architecture: Each layer has one primary responsibility

Wireless at Top: User interface far from robot

Wired at Bottom: Arduino Mega for real-time control (no network delays)

Sensor Fusion in Middle: Slave ESP32 processes sensor data before sending

Web-First UI: Browser-based control (no app download needed)

Redundancy: Can operate without internet (local Wi-Fi only)

This architecture allows you to control your garden robot from anywhere on your Wi-Fi network with real-time feedback!